        },
        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 1473,
            "sid": 3,
            "position_x": 729,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 702,
            "height": 661,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "!- subtracts every value that arrives at its left inlet FROM the operand. It is - with the two sides swapped: operand minus input.\n\n3 arriving at \"!- 10\" sends out 7 (10 minus 3), where \"- 10\" would send -7. A sine wave swinging from -1 to 1, sent through \"!- 0.5\", is turned upside down and lifted: it swings from 1.5 to -0.5.\n\nIts main use is INVERTING a range: \"!- 1.0\" turns a 0 to 1 control into 1 to 0, so one fader can bring one thing up while it takes another down; \"!- 127\" does the same for MIDI values. It also gives \"how much is left\": total minus amount used.\n\nWhy not just -? The changing value has to arrive at the left inlet, because that is the one that triggers. !- keeps that arrangement and subtracts the other way round.\n\nArrays and tensors are handled element by element, keeping their shape. A single-number operand applies to every element; an array operand of the same shape pairs element with element.\n\nSYNTAX:\n!- <operand: int or float>\n\nEXAMPLES:\n!- 1.0    turn a 0 to 1 control upside down\n!- 127    turn a MIDI value upside down\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe amount to subtract. Receiving data here triggers the calculation.\n\noperand:\nThe value subtracted from. \nSetting it does not send anything - it waits for the next value at \"in\". \"!- 1\" gives a stepped integer slider; \"!- 1.0\" a smooth floating point one.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe operand minus the input.\n\nSEE ALSO:\n-     the input minus the operand\n!/    the other swapped operation: the operand divided by the input",
                    "value_type": "string"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 694,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 645,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "string"
                }
            },
            "text": "!- subtracts every value that arrives at its left inlet FROM the operand. It is - with the two sides swapped: operand minus input.\n\n3 arriving at \"!- 10\" sends out 7 (10 minus 3), where \"- 10\" would send -7. A sine wave swinging from -1 to 1, sent through \"!- 0.5\", is turned upside down and lifted: it swings from 1.5 to -0.5.\n\nIts main use is INVERTING a range: \"!- 1.0\" turns a 0 to 1 control into 1 to 0, so one fader can bring one thing up while it takes another down; \"!- 127\" does the same for MIDI values. It also gives \"how much is left\": total minus amount used.\n\nWhy not just -? The changing value has to arrive at the left inlet, because that is the one that triggers. !- keeps that arrangement and subtracts the other way round.\n\nArrays and tensors are handled element by element, keeping their shape. A single-number operand applies to every element; an array operand of the same shape pairs element with element.\n\nSYNTAX:\n!- <operand: int or float>\n\nEXAMPLES:\n!- 1.0    turn a 0 to 1 control upside down\n!- 127    turn a MIDI value upside down\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe amount to subtract. Receiving data here triggers the calculation.\n\noperand:\nThe value subtracted from. \nSetting it does not send anything - it waits for the next value at \"in\". \"!- 1\" gives a stepped integer slider; \"!- 1.0\" a smooth floating point one.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe operand minus the input.\n\nSEE ALSO:\n-     the input minus the operand\n!/    the other swapped operation: the operand divided by the input"
        },
